class:: HarmonicSpread
summary:: Amplitude-weighted harmonic spectral spread
categories:: UGens>Analysis>Tonal
related:: Guides/MIRa, Classes/HPCP, Classes/HarmonicPeaks, Classes/HarmonicCentroid, Classes/FFTSpread2, Classes/Pitch2

description::
Shared interpolated spectral peaks are associated with the nearest integer harmonic of the supplied F0. Only the strongest matching peak occupies each harmonic slot. Missing harmonics retain zero slots; an incorrect F0 or tight tolerance changes the result. The formulas are explicit MIRa conventions in the harmonic-descriptor tradition summarized by Peeters (2004); this is independent code, not a wrapper around another analysis library. Computes the square root of sum(a_h * (f_h-centroid) squared) / sum(a_h), using the harmonic centroid.



classmethods::
method:: kr
argument:: chain
FFT chain. This analyzer updates when a new frame arrives and holds its output between frames.

argument:: f0
Supplied fundamental in Hz, for example the first output of Pitch2. Nonpositive or invalid values disable harmonic association.

argument:: maxHarmonics
Literal number of harmonic slots from 1 through 64. Harmonic index is stable when partials are missing.

argument:: tolerance
Maximum distance in cents between a peak and its nearest integer multiple of f0, clamped from 0 through 600. Default 50.

argument:: peakThreshold
Minimum local-peak magnitude relative to the largest included spectral peak, from 0 through 1. Up to 128 strongest peaks are retained.

argument:: fftSize
Literal power of two from 256 through 8192, matching the FFT buffer. Frequency resolution and first-result latency depend on this size.

returns::
Standard deviation in Hz; zero without associated peaks.

method:: fromAudio
Convenience constructor: accepts audio and builds Pitch2 plus FFT internally, then calls kr with the estimated F0. The input range defaults to 55 through 1760 Hz, FFT size 4096 and hop fraction 0.25; it accepts the same harmonic controls. Use kr when sharing an existing pitch estimate or FFT chain.

examples::
HarmonicSpread calculates the standard deviation of harmonic energy around the harmonic centroid in Hz. In this example, it drives an adaptive stereo widener: narrow, pure tones remain tightly focused in the center, while sounds with wide harmonic energy bloom out into an expansive stereo image via dynamic Haas delays. An interactive code::MIRaGUI.scope:: plots the harmonic spread in Hz.
code::
(
s.waitForBoot({
    var path = MIRaGUI.soundPath("melody.wav");
    ~miraBuffer = if(path.notNil) { Buffer.read(s, path) } { nil };
    ~miraBus = Bus.control(s, 1);
    s.sync;
    ~miraExample = {
        var source = if(~miraBuffer.notNil) {
            PlayBuf.ar(1, ~miraBuffer, BufRateScale.kr(~miraBuffer), loop: 1)
        } {
            // Synthetic melody fallback
            Saw.ar(Select.kr(LFNoise0.kr(2).range(0, 5), [220, 261.63, 293.66, 329.63, 392, 440])) * 0.2
        };
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);

        var pitch = PitchMPM.kr(source, 80, 1600, 2048, 0.25);
        var chain = FFT(LocalBuf(2048), source, 0.25);
        var spread = HarmonicSpread.kr(chain, pitch[0], maxHarmonics: 16, fftSize: 2048);
        var spreadNorm = (spread / 2500).clip(0, 1).lag(0.04);

        // Haas stereo widening driven by harmonic spread
        var delayL = DelayC.ar(source, 0.03, 0.001 + (spreadNorm * 0.015));
        var delayR = DelayC.ar(source, 0.03, 0.001 + ((1.0 - spreadNorm) * 0.015));
        var sound = ([source + (delayL * 0.5), source + (delayR * 0.5)]) * 0.25;

        if(~miraBus.notNil) { Out.kr(~miraBus, spread) };
        sound * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.2)
    }.play;

    ~miraGUI = MIRaGUI.scope("Harmonic Spread", ~miraBus, 0.0, 3000.0, "Hz");
});
)
::
code::
(
~miraExample.free;
if(~miraGUI.notNil) { ~miraGUI.close };
if(~miraBus.notNil) { ~miraBus.free };
if(~miraBuffer.notNil) { ~miraBuffer.free };
)
::
